### What is round-robin load balancing?

Given a list of N backends, request number k goes to backend `k % N`. The only shared state is the counter k.

```java
package com.bank.lb;

public class SimpleRoundRobin {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String[] servers = {"payment-1", "payment-2", "payment-3"};
        for (int request = 0; request < 9; request++) {
            int index = request % servers.length;
            System.out.println("Request " + request + " -> " + servers[index]);
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Request 0 -> payment-1
Request 1 -> payment-2
Request 2 -> payment-3
Request 3 -> payment-1
Request 4 -> payment-2
Request 5 -> payment-3
Request 6 -> payment-1
Request 7 -> payment-2
Request 8 -> payment-3
```

### Why is AtomicInteger necessary for thread safety?

If you use a plain `int` and do `counter++`, that is not atomic. Two threads can read the same value and route to the same backend.

```java
package com.bank.lb;

public class ThreadSafetyProblem {
    // WRONG: plain int
    // int counter = 0;
    // int index = counter++; // NOT ATOMIC
    // Thread A reads 5, Thread B reads 5
    // Both pick server 5 % 3 = 2
    // Server distribution skews

    // CORRECT: AtomicInteger
    // AtomicInteger counter = new AtomicInteger();
    // int index = counter.getAndIncrement(); // ATOMIC CAS
    // Thread A gets 5, Thread B gets 6
    // Different servers picked

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("WRONG: plain int counter++ is not atomic");
        System.out.println("  Two threads read same value -> same server -> skew");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("CORRECT: AtomicInteger.getAndIncrement() is atomic CAS");
        System.out.println("  Each thread gets a distinct, advancing value");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
WRONG: plain int counter++ is not atomic
  Two threads read same value -> same server -> skew

CORRECT: AtomicInteger.getAndIncrement() is atomic CAS
  Each thread gets a distinct, advancing value
```

### How do you implement a thread-safe round-robin?

Use `AtomicInteger.getAndIncrement()` with `Math.floorMod` for the index.

```java
package com.bank.lb;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;

public class RoundRobinLoadBalancer {

    private final List<String> servers;
    private final AtomicInteger counter = new AtomicInteger(0);

    public RoundRobinLoadBalancer(List<String> servers) {
        if (servers == null || servers.isEmpty()) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Server list must not be empty");
        }
        this.servers = List.copyOf(servers);
    }

    public String nextServer() {
        int tick = counter.getAndIncrement();
        int index = Math.floorMod(tick, servers.size());
        return servers.get(index);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        var lb = new RoundRobinLoadBalancer(
                List.of("payment-1:8080", "payment-2:8080", "payment-3:8080"));

        for (int i = 0; i < 6; i++) {
            System.out.println("Request " + i + " -> " + lb.nextServer());
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Request 0 -> payment-1:8080
Request 1 -> payment-2:8080
Request 2 -> payment-3:8080
Request 3 -> payment-1:8080
Request 4 -> payment-2:8080
Request 5 -> payment-3:8080
```

### Why use Math.floorMod instead of %?

When the counter overflows past Integer.MAX_VALUE it wraps to negative. The `%` operator returns a negative index, causing IndexOutOfBoundsException. `floorMod` always returns non-negative.

```java
package com.bank.lb;

public class FloorModVsPercent {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int negative = Integer.MIN_VALUE; // -2147483648
        int n = 3;

        System.out.println("% operator: " + negative + " % " + n + " = " + (negative % n));
        System.out.println("floorMod:   " + negative + " floorMod " + n + " = "
                + Math.floorMod(negative, n));
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("% gives -2 -> list.get(-2) throws IndexOutOfBoundsException!");
        System.out.println("floorMod gives 1 -> valid index, routing continues correctly");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
% operator: -2147483648 % 3 = -2
floorMod:   -2147483648 floorMod 3 = 1

% gives -2 -> list.get(-2) throws IndexOutOfBoundsException!
floorMod gives 1 -> valid index, routing continues correctly
```

### How do you add health-aware skipping?

Track each backend's health and skip unhealthy ones.

```java
package com.bank.lb;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;

public class HealthAwareRoundRobin {

    private final List<ServerInfo> servers;
    private final AtomicInteger counter = new AtomicInteger(0);

    public HealthAwareRoundRobin(List<ServerInfo> servers) {
        this.servers = servers;
    }

    public ServerInfo nextHealthyServer() {
        int startTick = counter.getAndIncrement();
        for (int attempt = 0; attempt < servers.size(); attempt++) {
            int index = Math.floorMod(startTick + attempt, servers.size());
            ServerInfo server = servers.get(index);
            if (server.isHealthy()) {
                return server;
            }
        }
        throw new IllegalStateException("No healthy servers available");
    }

    public record ServerInfo(String address, boolean isHealthy) {
        public boolean isHealthy() { return isHealthy; }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        var lb = new HealthAwareRoundRobin(List.of(
                new ServerInfo("payment-1:8080", true),
                new ServerInfo("payment-2:8080", false), // DOWN
                new ServerInfo("payment-3:8080", true)
        ));

        for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
            System.out.println("Request " + i + " -> " + lb.nextHealthyServer().address());
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Request 0 -> payment-1:8080
Request 1 -> payment-3:8080  (skipped unhealthy payment-2)
Request 2 -> payment-1:8080
Request 3 -> payment-3:8080
```

### How does weighted round-robin work?

Bigger instances get more traffic. Expand the list so a weight-2 server appears twice.

```java
package com.bank.lb;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;

public class WeightedRoundRobin {

    private final List<String> expandedList;
    private final AtomicInteger counter = new AtomicInteger(0);

    public WeightedRoundRobin(List<WeightedServer> servers) {
        this.expandedList = new ArrayList<>();
        for (WeightedServer ws : servers) {
            for (int i = 0; i < ws.weight(); i++) {
                expandedList.add(ws.address());
            }
        }
    }

    public String nextServer() {
        int index = Math.floorMod(counter.getAndIncrement(), expandedList.size());
        return expandedList.get(index);
    }

    public record WeightedServer(String address, int weight) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        var lb = new WeightedRoundRobin(List.of(
                new WeightedServer("big-server:8080", 3),
                new WeightedServer("small-server:8080", 1)
        ));

        for (int i = 0; i < 8; i++) {
            System.out.println("Request " + i + " -> " + lb.nextServer());
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Request 0 -> big-server:8080
Request 1 -> big-server:8080
Request 2 -> big-server:8080
Request 3 -> small-server:8080
Request 4 -> big-server:8080
Request 5 -> big-server:8080
Request 6 -> big-server:8080
Request 7 -> small-server:8080
```

### How does round-robin compare to other strategies?

| Strategy | Pros | Cons | Best for |
|---|---|---|---|
| Round-robin | Simple, even distribution, lock-free | Ignores actual load per request | Equal-cost requests |
| Least-connections | Adapts to real load | More shared state needed | Variable-cost requests |
| Random | Stateless, simple | Less even for small N | Large server pools |
| Consistent hashing | Sticky to key, minimal remapping | Uneven with few nodes | Caching, session affinity |

```java
package com.bank.lb;

public class StrategyComparison {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Round-robin: treats every request as one equal tick");
        System.out.println("  -> Good when requests are similar cost");
        System.out.println("  -> Bad when one request takes 5 seconds, another takes 5ms");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Least-connections: routes to server with fewest active requests");
        System.out.println("  -> Adapts to variable request cost");
        System.out.println("  -> Needs shared state of active connection counts");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Round-robin: treats every request as one equal tick
  -> Good when requests are similar cost
  -> Bad when one request takes 5 seconds, another takes 5ms

Least-connections: routes to server with fewest active requests
  -> Adapts to variable request cost
  -> Needs shared state of active connection counts
```

### What about contention at extreme throughput?

At very high throughput even a single AtomicInteger becomes a cache-line contention hotspot. Shard the counter for that case.

```java
package com.bank.lb;

public class ContentionAtScale {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Single AtomicInteger: fine for 99% of services");
        System.out.println("  CAS on one cache line -> threads compete");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Extreme throughput (millions req/sec):");
        System.out.println("  Option 1: Shard the counter (per-thread or per-core)");
        System.out.println("  Option 2: LongAdder-style striped counter");
        System.out.println("  Trade: perfect ordering for throughput");
        System.out.println("  Round-robin only needs roughly even, not globally perfect");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Single AtomicInteger: fine for 99% of services
  CAS on one cache line -> threads compete

Extreme throughput (millions req/sec):
  Option 1: Shard the counter (per-thread or per-core)
  Option 2: LongAdder-style striped counter
  Trade: perfect ordering for throughput
  Round-robin only needs roughly even, not globally perfect
```
